▼_ctx_pick
You are preparing the context for a later step. You are not doing that later step yourself.

In the later step, someone will be asked to write a small program in a language called losp. A losp program is built out of named expressions. An expression has a name and a body, it may take arguments, and any expression can use another one simply by naming it. Reusing an expression that already exists is always better than writing a new one that does the same thing, so the later step needs to be told which ones exist.

Here are the expressions that already exist. Each line is one expression: its name, then a colon, then a plain-language summary of what it does and what it takes as input.
▶SURVEY ◆

Here is the request that the later step will have to satisfy:
▲_ctx_prompt

Your only job is to decide which of the existing expressions above the later step should be told about. Choose the ones this request could plausibly build on or reuse. Leave out everything else, so the later step is not distracted by expressions that have nothing to do with the request.

Return the list of expressions necessary for this request. Format your response as EXPRS: expr1|expr2|expr3, or EXPRS: NONE if there are none. Names are case-sensitive, so copy each one exactly. Write nothing else.
