▼_ax_atomreq
You are breaking a job into its smallest steps, before anyone plans it or writes it.

Everything the job handles is text. A step reaches one value, works one value out from another, asks one question, or hands one piece of work on. Where the job does the same thing to every item of a list, write those steps once, for a single item. Say what comes back for that single item, and then say what comes back for every item.

These expressions already exist and can be used by name. Write each name exactly as it appears here, keeping the same capitalisation:
▲_ax_brief

When one of the expressions listed above already holds data a step needs, that step names it as where the data comes from, and no step is needed to obtain it.

Here is what the finished program has to do:
▶_ax_req ◆

List the steps in the order they happen, one step to a line, each short enough to say in one breath. Write no code.

Here are the steps of an unrelated job, to show their size and manner. That job was: named DutyList, it takes nothing and returns a line for each person on the rota at http://host/rota.txt saying whether they are on duty today.

download the text at http://host/rota.txt
set its heading line aside
take one line of the rota
reach the person's name in that line
reach the day that person works in that line
say on duty when that day is today's date
say off duty when it is not
join the person's name to that word
give back that joined line for the one line taken
give back one such line for every line of the rota
◆

▼_ax_planreq
You are planning a small program before anyone writes it.

The program will be written in a language called losp. A losp program is built out of named expressions. An expression has a name and a body, it may take arguments, and any expression can use another one simply by naming it. Several small expressions that each do one obvious thing are far easier to write correctly than one large expression that does everything at once.

These expressions already exist and can be used by name. Write each name exactly as it appears here, keeping the same capitalisation:
▲_ax_brief

Here is what the finished program has to do:
▶_ax_req ◆

That job has already been broken into its smallest steps, in the order they happen:
▲_ax_atoms

The finished program must end up in one expression named ▶_ax_name ◆, which is the expression that will actually be run.

What passes between expressions is always text, and a plan is clear when it says what kind of text. There are only these kinds:

a piece of text, which is what every expression takes and gives back;
a list, which is simply a piece of text of several lines, each line one item of it;
a line, which is one item of a list;
the fields of a line, which are its separated parts;
a name, which is where a value is kept so that it can be read back later.

Three facts about these settle the shape of any plan.

An expression takes one piece of text and gives one piece of text back. An expression whose real point is an effect still gives something back, so say what that is.

Work done to one item of a list has to be an expression with a name of its own, because naming it is the only way a list can be walked. So if this job does the same thing to every item of anything, it needs at least one helper beside ▶_ax_name ◆. If it does not, and it is small, ▶_ax_name ◆ may do the whole job alone.

A value made by one step and wanted by the next is described where it is used, as the result of the step that makes it. A value wanted in more than one place is kept under a name of its own, unique in the program to avoid clashes.

You are planning ▶_ax_name ◆. Write a block for ▶_ax_name ◆ itself, in this shape and nothing else:

EXPRESSION: ▶_ax_name ◆
TAKES: its one argument and what kind of text that is, or NOTHING
RETURNS: the text it gives back, named concretely; an expression that records or sends something gives text back too, so name that text
DOES: short plain sentences, one thing to each, in the order things happen, covering everything the request asks for and naming any helper it hands work to

Then a block below it, in that same shape, for each helper ▶_ax_name ◆ needs, so that every expression named in the plan has a block of its own. A name that only holds a value needs no block of its own.

The block for ▶_ax_name ◆ says in one sentence how the work is handed to the blocks it gathers. What comes back from that handing is its RETURNS, so its DOES needs no step for collecting or returning anything.

Here is such a plan, written for an unrelated request, to show the shape. That request was: named DutyList, it takes nothing and returns a line for each person on the rota at http://host/rota.txt saying whether they are on duty today.

EXPRESSION: DutyList
TAKES: NOTHING
RETURNS: one line for every person on the rota
DOES: it hands each line of RotaRows to RotaLine.

EXPRESSION: RotaFeed
TAKES: NOTHING
RETURNS: the whole rota, as a list of lines
DOES: it downloads the text at http://host/rota.txt.

EXPRESSION: RotaRows
TAKES: NOTHING
RETURNS: the rota's lines without its heading line
DOES: it gives back every line of RotaFeed after the first.

EXPRESSION: RotaPerson
TAKES: one line of the rota, whose fields are a person's name and the day they work
RETURNS: the person's name
DOES: it gives back the first field of the line.

EXPRESSION: RotaDay
TAKES: one line of the rota
RETURNS: the day that person works
DOES: it gives back the second field of the line.

EXPRESSION: RotaStatus
TAKES: one line of the rota
RETURNS: the words on duty, or the words off duty
DOES: it gives back on duty when RotaDay for that line is the same as today's date, and off duty when it is not.

EXPRESSION: RotaLine
TAKES: one line of the rota
RETURNS: one line saying whether that person is on duty
DOES: it gives back RotaPerson for that line, then a space, then RotaStatus for that line.

Give each expression one argument at most. Give every step above a block of its own, as the blocks here do, so that each block does one thing and stays short. A block that gathers work names the blocks it gathers rather than repeating what they do. Write no code.
◆

